WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.240
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Ross Douthat, who's a columnist at The New

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.550
York Times and also author of the recent book
The Decadent Society How We Became the Victims

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.520
of Our Own Success.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.690
Great to have you on, Ross.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:14.690
Thank you.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:15.690
Yeah, thanks for having me.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:16.690
It's a pleasure.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:22.130
So a lot of what you write about applies to
the situation in which we find ourselves today.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.611
But in order to kind of get into that conversation,
when you talk about a decadent society, what

00:00:26.611 --> 00:00:27.611
do you mean by that?

00:00:27.611 --> 00:00:30.199
Because it may it may not be obvious to some
in our audience.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:31.199
Right.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:35.490
So I'm trying to use the word decadence, not
to just mean, you know, chocolate covered

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:40.140
strawberries and, you know, bondage dens and
orgies and so on.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:46.540
But I'm trying to use it as a kind of technical
label for a stage that really successful civilizations

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.930
tend to reach when they become very rich and
powerful.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:58.510
And all of their sort of systems, governmental,
economic, are well established, hard to change.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.000
And moronic.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.930
You cannot essentially victims of their own
success.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.829
They're so powerful and rich that they stopped
doing the kind of things that you need to

00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:13.270
do to innovate both in politics and science
and culture.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.960
They stop having as many babies because, you
know, people have other things to do with

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:18.960
their time.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.159
And so they enter into periods of sort of
torpor, stagnation and repetition, which the

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:29.119
book argues we've been in at least up until,
you know, the current crisis that we entered

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:31.299
into while I was on my book tour.

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:37.159
How does this relate to some of the systems
that up until three months ago, many of us

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:43.579
maybe assumed were pretty strong and whose
fragility has actually been exposed by the

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:44.579
Corona virus?

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:51.890
Does that in any way play into this stagnation,
sort of that that you apply to economies that

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.409
in countries that are in this decadent phase
where we very quickly realized, hey, that

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:59.039
great looking stock market was actually not
quite so strong?

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:03.789
The supply chain for me, many consumer products
and even some food items was not nearly as

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:04.790
strong as we believed.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.420
Employment was not as strong as we believe.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.959
How does that play into this idea?

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:10.879
It plays in I mean, in a lot of different
ways.

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:13.019
But I'll give you a couple of examples.

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:17.629
One is, you know, the point that you made
about supply chains, one of the things that

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:23.390
the Western world has been doing basically
over the last 40 years is overall growth has

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.340
been slowing down.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.319
Growth hasn't been nearly as impressive as
it was, you know, in the heyday of the industrial

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:32.530
revolution or the postwar boom.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.739
And so we've been trying to essentially do
two things.

00:02:35.739 --> 00:02:41.040
One, spend way more money, way more public
money, basically, and take on way more debt

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.069
in order to sort of create the sense that
we're still growing quickly.

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:49.890
And too, we're trying to wring out sort of
maximal efficiencies everywhere we can find

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:55.349
them in order to, again, sort of goose growth
and with supply chains and, you know, things

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:59.830
like our our basic supplies of medical equipment
and masks and so on.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:04.849
You can see the danger in the second trend
where you're you know, you're so focused on

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:08.720
efficiency because you know that, you know,
you won't get strong growth without maximal

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.939
efficiency, that you aren't actually prepared
to be flexible when a real crisis comes along.

00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:16.440
And it turns out that efficiency isn't the
only good.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.390
It's also good to have stockpiles of medical
equipment.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.900
So that's one one sort of pattern.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.549
And another pattern is just the way sort of
large bureaucracies by their nature are built

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:29.290
to pursue safety rather than innovation.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:34.380
And that's not a bad thing for, say, obviously,
you want the FDA and the CDC to be focused

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.480
on making sure that medicine is safe and vaccines
are safe and so on.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.209
But it makes them inflexible, not particularly
adaptable.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:46.340
And you end up, in this case with a sort of
temporary catastrophe like the kind we had

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:51.829
with testing in this country over over the
critical two months when we missed the opportunity

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:57.609
to contain the virus, where the bureaucracy's
default mode is sort of the status quo is

00:03:57.609 --> 00:03:58.609
fine.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:03:59.609
And we just need to make sure everything's
safe.

00:03:59.609 --> 00:04:04.330
And when you actually have a new challenge,
you can't adapt to it particularly quickly.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:09.859
Now, when we think about the first element
you mentioned with the medical supplies, it

00:04:09.859 --> 00:04:15.811
doesn't seem to me as though the countries
that are not yet at a at a level of what you

00:04:15.811 --> 00:04:20.200
describe as decadence uniformly did better
on that issue.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.630
I mean, there was certainly a mix.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.650
There were countries that were more proactive.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.170
South Korea not not trying to Taiwan.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.110
I believe that within Europe.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:33.280
Greece certainly was one that moved earlier
and sort of more aggressively.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.270
But what other factors should we be looking
at?

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.560
Because it's not it can't simply be that it
was decadence that sort of prevented us from

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.490
being properly ready for that.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.139
No, it's also just immediate experience.

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:45.139
Right.

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:51.800
I mean, you have a clear division between
how quickly and seriously East Asian societies

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.680
and East Asian politics took this challenge.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.310
And how seriously the U.S. and Western Europe
took it, mostly because it's not just the

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.150
East Asian countries were in closer proximity
to China.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.300
They also had sort of direct experience of
stars, you know, in the immediate past.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:13.320
So they had sort of some knowledge and a sense
of how bad things could get and sort of, you

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.900
know, a sense of the need to act quickly in
a crisis.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.681
Whereas in the US and Western Europe, there
was a sense that, well, you know, something

00:05:21.681 --> 00:05:26.350
can happen in China and it must be, you know,
well, it's just a Chinese problem.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:30.190
It's the kind of thing that, you know, could
only happen in.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.290
You know what?

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:35.430
I think a lot of people in the West still
think of China as a developing country, a

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.470
country that sort of fundamentally different
from the developed world, which it is in certain

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.250
ways, but obviously not in others.

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:47.800
So there's just, I think, a level of sort
of recent experience and realism about the

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:54.229
real state of the world that was stronger
in East Asia than in the U.S. and Western

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:55.229
Europe.

00:05:55.229 --> 00:06:00.270
And I also think you could say in at a larger
level that there are ways in which generally

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:05.250
the economies of East Asia do look a little
less decadent generally than well, certainly

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:10.419
than Western Europe, but also than the U.S.
in terms of sort of their general willingness

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:16.699
to sort of embrace innovation, their sort
of state capacity, this term of art that people

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:21.170
used to refer to, you know, how good a government
is getting things done.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:26.660
State capacity seems like it's been in decline
in the US over the last few decades and maybe

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:27.860
less so in East Asia.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:30.860
So there's a lot of different dynamics going
on.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:37.850
So as we see in the in the United States,
even though at the country level we could

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.949
say we are certainly one of the richest countries
in the world.

00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:44.479
We have achieved a degree of what you describe
as decadence.

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:50.950
The reality about inequality means that for
a large number of people, only maybe a sliver

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:53.599
of some of these benefits are actually reaching
them.

00:06:53.599 --> 00:06:57.690
When you look at, you know, wage growth over
the last 40 years and how it disproportionately

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:03.819
has been for a small upper echelon of the
economy, so to speak, or of workers, as the

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:05.440
case may be.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.889
What is the relationship between a country
that top level has achieved the status that

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:18.630
you identify to the degree to which it uniformly
or not uniformly distributed amongst the people?

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.630
I think it depends.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:24.539
I think there are a couple of different ways
that you can get inequality.

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:27.720
And one is more decadent and one is less decadent
pride.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.599
So if you look at a period like late 19th
century America, you had soaring inequality,

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:36.990
you had the hero of the robber barons, the
great fortunes of the Rockefellers and Carnegies

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.520
were being made.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:45.479
And that created all kinds of social problems
that eventually sort of led to the labor movement,

00:07:45.479 --> 00:07:48.840
the populist and progressive era, the New
Deal.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.740
But it wasn't an inequality that really reflected
decadence.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:56.889
It was an inequality that reflected this age
of innovation where you had sudden innovations.

00:07:56.889 --> 00:08:02.120
A few people were capturing the returns and
it took a while for those returns to spread

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.939
throughout the population.

00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:09.009
Our own era, the last 30 years or so, it looks
like that a little bit with Silicon Valley

00:08:09.009 --> 00:08:14.810
where you have, you know, Internet innovation,
de Zuckerbergs and so on, get rich.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.919
And it doesn't spread out to the rest of society.

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:24.990
But a lot of what we've seen outside the Internet
economy is a stagnant general stagnation,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:29.120
slowing rates of growth where rich people
aren't getting rich by embracing innovative

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:30.120
new technologies.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:34.570
They're getting rich just through the power
of investments and compound interest and so

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:35.570
on.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:39.440
And this is and Thomas Piketty, this is sort
of the argument is this is not this is the

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.230
more libertarian, in a way, version of the
argument that he made in his book, Capital

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:49.890
in the 21st Century, where he basically said,
you know, as societies sought, as growth slows

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:54.731
down and societies get older and people have
fewer kids, great fortunes become more and

00:08:54.731 --> 00:08:57.940
more consolidated and rich people live off
ramps.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:03.709
And that kind of inequality is more decadent
and more stagnant and less likely to ultimately

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:08.560
benefit society as a whole than the inequality
you get when you know people are building

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.580
the transcontinental railroad and a lot of
opportunists are getting rich off that innovation.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.769
One of the things you talk about in the book
is the degree to which a lot of the same political

00:09:18.769 --> 00:09:23.300
arguments just continue to be had over and
over again with no real progress.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.160
Once a country reaches this level of decadence.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.461
And I think we can intuitively see that if
we follow politics, a lot of issues that are

00:09:30.461 --> 00:09:34.510
merely political footballs, you know, debt
and deficit, just kind of tossing it back

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:35.510
and forth.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:39.680
What should the role of the United States
military be around the world, do low taxes

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.930
on the rich, trickle down, or do we need demand
sites that, you know, a lot of these it's

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.779
the same arguments over and over and over
again.

00:09:45.779 --> 00:09:51.890
Can you talk about why the repetitive nature
of these arguments is reinforced by the decadence

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:52.890
you write about?

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.070
I mean, I think debt.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:10:00.700
Creates a world in which, I mean, among other
things.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.630
There because there isn't tremendous change
going on.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.340
There aren't sort of it.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:11.440
It becomes hard to win an argument to sort
of decisively win one way or another.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.399
Systems are really hard to reform and change.

00:10:13.399 --> 00:10:14.459
So, you know, you'll get.

00:10:14.459 --> 00:10:18.810
Barack Obama will be elected president and
everyone will say, oh, this is you know, the

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.760
new new deal is a new Franklin Roosevelt.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.380
You're gonna have a new age of liberal governance.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:29.520
And he passes Obamacare, which is sort of
a jerry rigged apparatus tacked on to an existing

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.860
welfare state passing.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.050
It is incredibly difficult and politically
costly and you get a huge backlash.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:40.740
And effectively, his presidency as a legislative
force is over two years in and then immediately

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.450
the Tea Party sweeps in and everyone says,
well, it's a new libertarian era.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.450
And, you know, Paul Ryan is the new you know,
he's the new great reformer.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.890
And he's going to, you know, undo the Great
Society or something.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:56.620
But that doesn't actually go anywhere because
people don't particularly want to undo the

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.620
Great Society.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:02.329
And so you have you know, the libertarian
moment quickly turns into stalemate, too.

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:07.610
So it's a combination of society's get older,
they're more resistant to change big programs,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:10.970
long standing systems are more impervious
to change.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:15.570
And so people argue at the margins, they argue
about a thirty nine percent top tax rate versus

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:20.521
a 33 percent top tax rate, even while their
rhetoric makes it sound like, you know, it's

00:11:20.521 --> 00:11:24.730
the great clash between capitalism and socialism.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:26.329
And so that's been the pattern.

00:11:26.329 --> 00:11:31.730
Certainly, I think of the last of of the last
twenty five years or so.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:35.960
The question is whether it's still holds or
whether populism on the right and Bernie Sanders

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:41.220
style socialism on the left are actually going
to sort of shift the underlying dynamics.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.920
But I don't think they've actually shift in
policy debates yet, even if they've shifted

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.510
arguments somewhat.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.070
So you talk about candidates as a possible
way to shift this.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:57.100
What about your writing that there could be
a crisis moment that actually fix things up?

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:02.230
Is the global pandemic we're dealing with
now potentially on the level of what you're

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.790
talking about, or is it not even big enough?

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.940
I mean, it might not be big enough.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:13.680
Well, which is one of the things one of the
points I make in the book is that decadence

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.070
has all kinds of problems.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:20.990
But sometimes that kind of crisis you need
to really shift a society out of decadence

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:26.990
is so big that it's better to stick with that
kid and to embrace that catastrophe.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:30.160
But look, what the Corona virus offers is
an opportunity.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:36.339
You know, it's a it's a horrible human tragedy
that in its aftermath there will be an opportunity

00:12:36.339 --> 00:12:40.769
to look at all the ways our systems have failed,
to look at all the ways our politics has become

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:46.550
stalemated and didn't handle the crisis correctly
and say, you know, maybe we can do something

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:47.660
new.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:52.470
And that could lead to, you know, you could
have an era when people say, OK, well, we

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:56.910
really are going to, you know, change how
regulations work in the health care sector

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.970
to try and get more medical innovation.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.769
You can get a world where people say, look,
we spent all this money in a crisis.

00:13:02.769 --> 00:13:06.600
Why can't we spend more money on public goods
when there isn't a pandemic around?

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.370
And so you get a shift to the left in public
policy.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.480
You can imagine a lot of shifts coming out
of this.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:19.100
But I do think it's possible that, you know,
we will have this this year of turmoil and

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:23.269
then we will get a vaccine and there'll be
sort of this sense of relief that things are

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:28.149
back to normal and people will have sort of
gone deeper into decadence in certain ways,

00:13:28.149 --> 00:13:34.980
like, you know, small businesses, new entrepreneurship,
which have been sort of smushed down big companies

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:40.930
will benefited the most from the bailout packages
and from the Fed's policies until you'll get

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:45.360
more consolidation, more big companies that
aren't particularly innovative and so on.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:49.480
You know, more people living their lives online
instead of instead of getting married and

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:50.480
starting families.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:54.820
You can imagine a lot of ways in which this
deepens decadence potentially even as it offers

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:56.570
a chance to pull out of it.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:57.570
A couple of weeks ago.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.699
I interviewed Brian GREENE.

00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:03.259
And one of the things we talked about because
it kind of mixes in well with a few different

00:14:03.259 --> 00:14:08.639
things he deals with, was this idea that that
I have, which increasingly I think is naive,

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:14.420
that if there was some really life-altering
moment, like, for example, intelligent alien

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.020
life arrives.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:17.050
Right.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:23.339
Something really transcendent that it would
finally get humans to do away with these differences

00:14:23.339 --> 00:14:28.250
that that as you talk about often or the difference
between a thirty six and a thirty nine point

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:33.480
six percent top tax rate or whatever, we would
finally get past all this stuff.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:38.910
But I have to say that the fiasco that has
been the reaction to Corona virus, where you

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:44.800
have people who who you know, all sorts of
conspiracies and you all we don't even have

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:49.920
to go into it, you know, it that has made
me think that even something like intelligent

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:54.339
alien life, maybe for a month would would
get us to rethink some of our differences.

00:14:54.339 --> 00:15:00.019
Then they'll all just kind of go back to normal
to the way things have been.

00:15:00.019 --> 00:15:01.019
I don't know.

00:15:01.019 --> 00:15:05.320
Well, I mean, the amusing thing is that we
have literally had some sort of footage of

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.980
not probably not actual aliens, but at the
very least, UFOs dropped in the middle of

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:11.980
the coronavirus crisis.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:12.980
Right.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:18.240
You had the Navy releasing footage of an unidentified
flying objects.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:23.930
And, you know, except for a few people I follow
on Twitter, almost nobody has cared.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:28.590
But just to be clear that those are not being
presented as evidence of aliens.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:29.590
Right.

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.980
But but regardless, you would think that that
entire community would have paid more attention.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:34.350
They're there, right?

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:38.760
They're being presented, but they are they
are very mysterious objects.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:43.950
They go beyond, I think, this sort of normal
range of sort of grainy footage of UFOs.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.860
They are probably not aliens, but they certainly
are really interesting.

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:53.050
And in a different moment, you would imagine
that sort of capturing the imagination of

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:54.050
the Internet.

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:57.839
But really, the Internet is more interested
in, you know, figuring out ways to blame everything

00:15:57.839 --> 00:16:02.029
about the Corona virus on on people that they
dislike.

00:16:02.029 --> 00:16:08.000
We've been speaking with Ross Douthat, a columnist
at The New York Times and also author recently

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.220
of The Decadent Society How We Became the
Victims of Our Own Success.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:15.389
Ross, I so appreciate your time.

00:16:15.389 --> 00:16:16.389
Thanks so much for having me.

00:16:16.389 --> 00:16:16.800
It was a pleasure.

